      "en": "Once a required stop on caravan routes that brought Asian goods west to eager Romans, Palmyra has \"always been conceived as an oasis in the middle of the desert, but it's never been quite clear what it was living from,\" said Michal Gawlikowski, the retired head of the University of Warsaw's Polish Mission at Palmyra. And what an oasis: Among the ruins are grand avenues lined with columns, triumphal arches, and the remains of an ancient market where traders once haggled over silk, silver, spices, and dyes from India and China. The landscape around the city, it now appears, was intensively farmed and most likely included olive, fig, and pistachio groves-crops known in the region from Roman accounts and still common in Syria. Barley too was grown, according to a pollen analysis Meyer's team conducted on a mud brick from the survey area. *(National Geographic)*",
