      "en": "Lafarge and Holcim are leading players in construction materials like cement, gravel and asphalt, which are used to pave roads. Because the cement market is largely local—cement is cheap to make but expensive to transport—both companies have expanded in many of the same markets around the world. The companies' combined market share of cement-making capacity is around 60% in France, Canada and Morocco and 30% in the U.S. The two companies have combined annual sales of nearly $43 billion, dwarfing rivals such as Germany's HeidelbergCement, with $18 billion in sales, and Mexico's Cemex, with $15 billion. The European Commission has been investigating Holcim, Lafarge and six other companies for operating a suspected cartel in cement and cement-based products since December 2010. *(online.wsj.com)*",
